## 0. Orientation: what the paper actually claims

The paper makes one structural observation and builds an algorithm around it.

**The observation.** The congestion component of a nodal LMP vector is, by the KKT conditions of the market clearing problem, a linear combination of specific rows of the PTDF matrix â€” precisely the rows belonging to the transmission lines that are binding at that instant. Because only a handful of lines bind during any short market interval, the congestion-price vector is confined to a *low-dimensional linear subspace*, and the identity of the binding set is exactly the identity of that subspace. Over time, as generators' marginal offers fluctuate, the shadow prices on the binding lines wander, so the congestion vector moves *within* its subspace but never leaves it. A time series of congestion prices is therefore a cloud of points sampled from a **union of subspaces**, one subspace per congestion pattern (per "system pattern region," SPR).

**The task.** Recover the congestion status at every interval â€” i.e., cluster the points into their subspaces and recover the spanning (basis) vectors â€” using *only the public LMP data*, with no access to topology, line parameters, PTDF, or nodal loads. A useful by-product: the recovered basis vectors are (up to sign and scale) the PTDF rows of the binding lines, so you reconstruct part of the PTDF matrix.

**Why it's hard.** The subspaces *overlap and nest*: if line A alone spans a 1-D subspace and line B alone spans another, then A-and-B-together span the 2-D subspace that *contains both*. Off-the-shelf subspace clustering (GPCA, sparse subspace clustering) assumes independent or disjoint subspaces and breaks on this nesting, and there are outliers. The contribution is a bespoke hierarchical search â€” bottom-up peeling plus top-down hyperplane detection â€” that handles nested subspaces with outliers.

The rest of this report walks every section, develops the mathematics, then adds a critical assessment and the connection to regional-market (NEM-style) settings.

