## 3. Section II â€” Model and distribution of LMP

This section derives *why* the subspace structure exists, first without losses, then with, then illustrates the geometry.

### 3.1 Lossless DC-OPF and the LMP decomposition

The clearing problem is
$$
\min_{P_G}\ \sum_{i\in\mathcal N} c_i(P_{Gi})
\quad\text{s.t.}\quad
\mathbf 1^\top P_G = \mathbf 1^\top P_D\ (\lambda),\quad
|T(P_G-P_D)|\le f^{\max}\ (\mu^\pm),\quad
P^{\min}\le P_G\le P^{\max}\ (\gamma).
$$
The LMP is the sensitivity of the optimal cost to demand, i.e., the derivative of the Lagrangian w.r.t. $P_D$:
$$
\boxed{\ \pi = \lambda\mathbf 1 + T^\top\mu,\qquad \mu = \mu^- - \mu^+\ }
$$
which splits cleanly into
$$
\pi^{\mathcal E} = \lambda\mathbf 1 \quad(\text{energy: identical at every node}),\qquad
\pi^{\mathcal C} = T^\top\mu \quad(\text{congestion}).
$$
The decisive fact is complementary slackness: $\mu_j \neq 0$ **only for a binding line** $j$. Writing $T_j$ for the $j$-th column of $T^\top$ (= the $j$-th PTDF row),
$$
\pi^{\mathcal C} = \sum_{j\ \text{binding}} \mu_j\, T_j \ \in\ \mathrm{span}\{T_j : j\ \text{binding}\}.
$$
So the congestion vector lives in the span of the PTDF rows of the currently binding lines. The *binding set* is the *subspace*. As offers fluctuate, the $\mu_j$ change magnitude â€” the point slides around inside the span â€” but the span itself is fixed by which lines bind. That is the entire mechanism the paper exploits.

### 3.2 Lossy DC-OPF (Litvinov et al. model)

Real ISOs price losses. Adding a linearized loss balance and redistributing losses in the flow constraints:
$$
\mathbf 1^\top P_G = \mathbf 1^\top P_D + l\ (\lambda),\qquad
l = l_0 + LF^\top(P_G-P_D)\ (\sigma),\qquad
|T(P_G-P_D-l\,d)|\le f_{\max}\ (\mu^\pm).
$$
The LMP becomes
$$
\pi = \sigma\mathbf 1 - \sigma\,LF + T^\top\mu - \mathbf 1\!\cdot\!(d^\top T^\top\mu),
$$
now a three-way split:
$$
\pi^{\mathcal E} = \sigma\mathbf 1,\qquad
\pi^{\mathcal C} = \underbrace{T^\top\mu}_{\pi_1} - \underbrace{\mathbf 1\,(d^\top T^\top\mu)}_{\pi_2},\qquad
\pi^{\mathcal L} = -\sigma\,LF.
$$

**The loss wrinkle and its fix.** The nuisance is $\pi_2$: because the loss distribution vector $d$ is refreshed frequently, $\pi_2$ is *not* built from constant vectors, and it contaminates the clean subspace geometry of $\pi_1 = T^\top\mu$. The saving grace: $d^\top T^\top\mu$ is a **scalar**, so $\pi_2 = \mathbf 1\cdot(\text{scalar})$ adds the *same* value at every node. It is annihilated by subtracting the congestion price at any single reference node from all nodes. After that de-meaning, $\pi^{\mathcal C}$ collapses back to $\pi_1$ and the subspace structure is restored. This one-line preprocessing is what lets the method run on genuinely lossy markets (used on SPP in Case 4).

### 3.3 Sparsity â†’ subspace, and the toy example

ISOs publish all three LMP components. Empirically only a tiny fraction of lines bind in any short window â€” in CAISO, typically **two lines** are congested at a time. Hence $\pi^{\mathcal C}$ always sits in a subspace spanned by a *few* columns of $T^\top$ (lossless) or of $T^\top - \mathbf 1 d^\top T^\top$ (lossy).

The 3-bus example makes it visible: bus 1 as reference, small Gaussian noise on the two generators' marginal costs, loads at buses 2 and 3 swept over $[-150, 300]$ MWh. Under the lossless model the congestion vectors fall onto exactly **two lines through the origin** â€” two congestion statuses, trivially separable by eye.

### 3.4 The real difficulty â€” overlapping subspaces

Beyond four nodes the geometry can't be drawn, but the schematic (their Fig. 3) captures the essential complication. Suppose subspaces $S_1, S_2, S_3$ each correspond to a single congested line, and $S_4$ to two lines congested together â€” the two lines of $S_1$ and $S_2$. Then $\pi^{\mathcal C}\in S_4$ whenever both of those lines bind, and crucially **$S_4$ entirely contains $S_1$ and $S_2$**. Standard subspace-clustering methods assume independent or disjoint subspaces; they can separate $S_3$ from $S_4$ but cannot resolve $S_4$ nesting $S_1$ and $S_2$. This containment â€” plus outliers â€” is exactly what forces a specialized algorithm, and it motivates the hierarchical (bottom-up-then-top-down) design in Section III.

### 3.5 Robustness to modern SCED (forward reference)

Multi-interval and look-ahead SCED add ramping and other inter-temporal constraints, which change the *values* of $\mu$ and $\sigma$. The authors note â€” and prove in the Appendix â€” that the *functional form* of the LMP decomposition is unchanged, so the subspace argument and the whole method survive these market designs. (Details in Â§8 below.)

