## 4. Section III â€” Methodology

### 4.1 (A) Problem formulation

Stack the congestion vectors into a data matrix
$$
X = [\pi^{\mathcal C}_t]_{t\in\mathcal T},\qquad |\mathcal T| = M .
$$
Let $j_1,\dots,j_k$ ($k \ll n_l$) index the lines that ever congest over the window, and $T_1,\dots,T_{n_b}$ be the columns of $T^\top$. Then every column of $X$ is a combination of the same $k$ PTDF rows:
$$
X = [x_1,\dots,x_M] = [\,T_{j_1},\dots,T_{j_k}\,]\cdot[\chi_t]_{t\in\mathcal T},
$$
where $\chi_t\in\mathbb R^k$ holds the binding-line shadow prices $\mu$ at time $t$. The Boolean support
$$
\mathfrak X_t = \mathbb 1(\chi_t\neq 0)\approx \mathbb 1(|\chi_t|>\epsilon)
$$
**is** the congestion status: it says which of the $k$ candidate lines are binding at $t$. There are $2^k$ possible statuses in principle, but only a few materialize. In the 30-bus test, over 4 candidate lines (#10, 29, 30, 35) only **9** distinct statuses appear (their Table I), ranging from single-line patterns like $(0,0,1,0)$ up to the all-four $(1,1,1,1)$. Recovering $\mathfrak X_t$ from $X$ *without knowing $T$* is the whole game.

### 4.2 (B) Basic framework

Since all $M$ columns share the common spanning set $\{T_{j_1},\dots,T_{j_k}\}$, it suffices to find a common basis $B$. The pipeline: (i) PCA to drop the ambient dimension from $n_b$ to $k$ losslessly; (ii) a bottom-up search for the *explicit* basis vectors; (iii) if a "basis gap" blocks the bottom-up search, a top-down search to recover the *implicit* ones; (iv) once $B$ is in hand, encode $\mathfrak X_t = \mathbb 1(B^\dagger x_t\neq 0)$.

### 4.3 (C) Principal component analysis

$X$ has rank $k$ (tiny relative to $n_b$), so PCA compresses it to $k$ components with **no information loss** â€” purely to cut the cost of the downstream linear algebra. Complexity $O(n_b^2 M + n_b^3)$. (Notation is then abused to keep calling the reduced matrix $X$.)

### 4.4 (D) Recursive basis search â€” bottom-up

**Why start at dimension 1.** Finding arbitrary nested subspaces of mixed dimension in $\mathbb R^k$ is hard; finding *1-D* subspaces (rays through the origin) is easy, because two vectors lie on the same ray iff their cosine similarity is $\pm 1$:
$$
\cos\angle(v_1,v_2) = \frac{v_1^\top v_2}{\|v_1\|\,\|v_2\|} = 1 .
$$

**Building a clean affinity graph.** Normalize the columns and form the cosine-similarity affinity $A = |X^\top X|$. Then apply a hard **cutoff kernel**
$$
\kappa(x) = \begin{cases}1 & |x|>1-\varepsilon\\[2pt]0 & \text{otherwise}\end{cases}
$$
to get a sparse $A' = \kappa(A)$ that retains edges *only* between near-collinear vectors. Now "find 1-D subspaces" $\equiv$ "find connected clusters in $A'$."

**Choosing the number of clusters automatically.** Apply spectral clustering with the **relative-eigengap** rule. Order the eigenvalues of $A'$ ascending, $\gamma_1\le\cdots\le\gamma_M$, and compute
$$
\xi_i = \frac{\gamma_{i+1}-\gamma_i}{\gamma_i},\qquad i\ge 2 .
$$
A large $\xi_i$ signals that $A'$ admits a good decomposition into at least $i$ clusters; run $k$-means with that many partitions on the leading eigenvectors, then read off the basis vector of each rank-1 cluster.

**Peeling a dimension (the recursive step).** This is the heart of bottom-up. Suppose $v_1, v_2$ live in the 2-D subspace $\mathrm{span}(b_1,b_2)$ and $v_3$ elsewhere, and spectral clustering has already recovered $b_1$. **Orthogonalize** every point against $b_1$ â€” project onto $\mathrm{span}(b_1)^\perp$ â€” giving $\bar v_1, \bar v_2, \bar v_3$. After this projection, $\bar v_1$ and $\bar v_2$ are *collinear* (they now lie on a 1-D ray), so $b_2$ falls out of another spectral-clustering pass. Formerly-2-D subspaces have become 1-D. Repeat: each round strips one dimension off the whole cloud and exposes the next layer of basis vectors. Terminate when only one cluster remains ($K=1$).

**Algorithm 1 (bottom-up), in words.** Repeat until $K=1$: normalize $X$; form $A=|X^\top X|$ and $A'=\kappa(A,\varepsilon)$; spectral-cluster into $X_1,\dots,X_K$; for each cluster, if $\mathrm{Rank}(X_i)=1$ add its basis to $\mathcal B$; project $X$ onto $\mathcal B^\perp$ (dimension reduction). Return $\mathcal B$. Complexity: affinity $O(k^2M)$, full SVD for spectral clustering $O(M^3)$, then $k$-means.

**When bottom-up stalls â€” the basis gap.** Bottom-up needs a "ladder" that begins at dimension 1: at least one observed pattern with a *single* line binding, so that some points sit on a genuine ray. If **every** observed status has $\ge 2$ lines binding, no point ever lies in a 1-D subspace, spectral clustering finds no rank-1 cluster, and the peeling never starts. That failure mode is the **basis gap** (their Fig. 6). It is not a pathology â€” it is the norm in tightly meshed regions where lines only ever co-congest. The remedy is to attack from the top.

### 4.5 (E) Hyperplane detection â€” top-down

**The problem.** Instead of building up from rays, find a **hyperplane** $H_1$ (codimension 1, i.e., rank $k-1$) that contains as many points as possible. Equivalently, find its unit normal $n$ orthogonal to the largest cluster of points:
$$
\min_n\ \|X^\top n\|_0 \quad\text{s.t.}\quad \|n\|_2 = 1 .
$$
This is nonconvex on two counts (the $\ell_0$ count and the sphere constraint). Any solver lands in one of three regimes: (1) **global optimum** â€” $H_1$ holds the most points; (2) **non-trivial local optimum** â€” $H_1$ holds enough points to clear a threshold, which is *acceptable*; (3) **trivial** â€” too few points, rejected. The key relaxation of ambition: you do **not** need the global optimum, because any hyperplane containing a substantial point mass corresponds to a real congestion status.

**Solving it with DPCP.** Use Dual Principal Component Pursuit. First relax $\ell_0 \to \ell_1$:
$$
\min_n\ \|X^\top n\|_1 \quad\text{s.t.}\quad \|n\|_2 = 1 ,
$$
whose constraint is still nonconvex, then solve a **sequence of linear programs** that linearize the sphere:
$$
n_{i+1} := \operatorname*{argmin}_{m^\top \hat n_i = 1}\ \|X^\top m\|_1,\qquad i\in\mathbb N,
$$
where $\hat{\ \cdot\ }$ denotes $\ell_2$-normalization and $n_0$ is initialized to minimize $\|X^\top n\|_2$ (the smallest right-singular vector). At convergence, inliers vs. outliers separate by their inner product with $n$; when the outlier fraction is small, the iterates reach the global optimum of the $\ell_1$ problem.

**Why DPCP over alternatives.** EP-SpUD and ADM-based sparse-vector methods also target this problem, but none solves it perfectly for arbitrary $X$. DPCP is chosen because it holds up in the *challenging regime of large outlier ratios and high relative subspace dimension* â€” exactly the LMP regime, where the "inliers" of one hyperplane are a minority and everything else is an outlier. Concretely, with full-rank dimension $k$, $H_1$ of rank $k-1$, and inlier proportion $q$, DPCP provably tolerates on the order of
$$
O\!\left(\frac{q^2 M^2}{(k-1)\,k\,\log k^2}\right)\ \text{outliers}.
$$

**Random-sampling fallback.** When inliers are too sparse for DPCP to certify a result, switch to random sampling (RS): draw $k-1$ columns $x_{r_1},\dots,x_{r_{k-1}}$, build the normal $\tilde n$ of their span by Gaussian elimination, and accept it iff
$$
\Theta(X^\top\tilde n) > pM
$$
(the hyperplane contains at least a fraction $p$ of the points; $\Theta$ counts zeros, i.e., orthogonal points). Otherwise resample, up to $N$ draws. The probability of drawing $k-1$ inliers in one shot is
$$
\frac{\binom{qM}{k-1}}{\binom{M}{k-1}} \approx q^{k-1}\quad(k-1\ll qM),
$$
so failure after $N$ draws is $\approx (1-q^{k-1})^N$, which **decays exponentially in $N$**. Choosing $N$ to hold the failure probability below $\varepsilon$ gives RS cost $O(Nk^3)\approx O\!\big(-\log\varepsilon\cdot(1/p)^{k-1}k^3\big)$. Note the practical sting: the single-draw success $q^{k-1}$ collapses fast when the pattern is rare (small $q$) or the subspace is high-dimensional (large $k$).

**Algorithm 2 (top-down), in words.** Try $\tilde n \leftarrow \mathrm{DPCP}(X)$; if condition (16) holds, return it; else loop up to $N$ times: $\tilde n \leftarrow \mathrm{RS}(X)$; return the first that satisfies (16). One call recovers one hyperplane; you then recurse into both the discovered hyperplane and its outlier set.

**Why bottom-up goes first.** The top-down recursion is a tree â€” recurse into inliers and into outliers â€” with up to $2^k$ nodes in the worst case (their Fig. 10). Running bottom-up first shaves off whatever explicit basis vectors exist and reduces the working dimension, shrinking the tree and improving overall efficiency. The two searches are complementary: bottom-up is cheap and exact when a dimensional ladder exists; top-down is the general fallback that bridges basis gaps.

### 4.6 (F) The assembled pipeline

1. **Preprocess** â€” drop non-congestion columns; PCA to dimension $k$.
2. **Bottom-up** â€” run Algorithm 1 to get basis set $\mathcal B$.
3. **Project & prune** â€” project $X$ onto $\mathcal B^\perp$, delete all-zero columns, call the remainder $X^{(2)}$. *Usually $X^{(2)}$ is empty and you stop here.*
4. **Top-down (if needed)** â€” if $X^{(2)}$ is non-empty, iterate Algorithm 2 to recover the remaining subspaces via their normals.
5. **Decode** â€” assemble $B$ from the bottom-up bases and the top-down normals. If $\mathrm{Rank}(B)=\mathrm{Rank}(X)$, the basis is complete; recover the coefficients
$$
\hat\chi_t = B^\dagger X\quad(\text{Mooreâ€“Penrose pseudoinverse}),
$$
and threshold to get $\mathfrak X_t$.

