## 5. Section IV â€” Numerical experiments

**Common setup.** Intel Core i7-7500U @ 2.90 GHz, MATLAB R2016a; market cleared every 5 minutes; **576** congestion vectors collected per synthetic case. Accuracy is the **miscode rate**
$$
\textit{misrate} = 1 - \sum_{t\in\mathcal T}\frac{\Theta(\mathfrak X_t - \mathfrak C_t)}{k\times M},
$$
where $\mathfrak C_t = \mathbb 1(B^\dagger x_t)$ is the recovered code â€” i.e., the fraction of *line-status bits* recovered incorrectly, reportable per line or in total.

### Case 1 â€” IEEE 30-bus (bottom-up demonstration)

Six generators (buses 1, 2, 13, 22, 23, 27) with block offers as in Kekatos et al.; loads from a Kaggle 2008 dataset; 3% relative-std Gaussian noise on both offers and loads. There is **no basis gap**, so bottom-up suffices. After PCA, $X$ reduces to shape $(4, 576)$, exposing $k=4$ ever-congested lines. With $\varepsilon=0.005$, Round 1 finds 4 clusters, of which clusters 1 and 3 are rank-1 â†’ two bases $b_1, b_2$. Projecting onto $\mathcal B^\perp$ leaves $X^{(2)}$ with 30 points, so Round 2 runs and finds 2 more clusters â†’ $\mathrm{Rank}(B)=4$. Decoding gives a very low miscode rate (Table III: **0.39% total**; the individual lines are 0, 0, 1.22%, 0.35%). This case exists to visualize the peeling: cluster â†’ extract â†’ orthogonalize â†’ repeat.

### Case 2 â€” IEEE 118-bus (top-down demonstration)

Regional loads and participation factors from the NREL extended 118-bus case (Dec 2012); thermal limits imposed on **7 interregional lines**; offers set so Region 1 is the costliest of three; 3% Gaussian noise in the real-time market. The observed statuses (Table II) *always* have $\ge 2$ lines binding â€” e.g., $(1,0,1,1)$ at 51.9% and $(1,0,1,0)$ at 39.9% frequency across lines #(44, 45, 54, 128) â€” so bottom-up finds **no rank-1 cluster**: a textbook basis gap. Top-down then runs (their Fig. 10):

- Round 1: DPCP succeeds, normal $\tilde n_1$; **529 of 576** points lie in that hyperplane, and the search continues within them; 47 points are outliers.
- Round 2: DPCP fails on the 529; RS recovers $\tilde n_2$ with **230** orthogonal points. Separately, RS on the 47 Round-1 outliers yields $\tilde n_3$ with **19** orthogonal points.
- Round 3: both DPCP and RS return negative â†’ the bottom of the subspace hierarchy is reached.

The basis is then read off the tree: $b_1, b_2$ from the 230-point set, $b_3$ from the other 299 points (rank 3), $b_4$ from the 19-point set. **Total miscode rate 0.**

### Case 3 â€” Illinois 200-bus (scale test)

Loads randomized around the MATPOWER standard case; offers as two blocks of 65% capacity at 30 and 35 \$/MWh; **6 of 245 lines** ever congest. A basis gap exists again; top-down (DPCP + RS, iterated) runs **5 rounds** to recover 6 bases. **Total miscode rate 0** â€” the method scales to a 200-bus system without accuracy loss.

### Case 4 â€” Southwest Power Pool (real market data)

Real-time LMPs from **7,408 nodes** on **December 7, 2018**. Nodes that differ only in name and share identical LMPs throughout are merged, leaving **3,300** nodes. SPP publishes real-time $\pi^{\mathcal C}$ together with the binding constraints and their shadow prices $\mu$ (so ground truth is available for validation). Of **288** intervals, **249** have one or more binding constraints; **6** lines congest during the day. Because SPP uses a **lossy** model, the raw $\pi^{\mathcal C}$ is first de-meaned to strip $\pi_2$ (their Fig. 11 shows intervals 3 and 5 â€” both single-line â€” collapsing to nearly identical nodal congestion-price ratios after the correction, confirming the fix works). Then bottom-up runs: Round 1 finds 2 rank-1 clusters of sizes 147 and 17 â†’ 2 bases, leaving 85 intervals; Round 2 finds 3 clusters (sizes 39, 20, 10); Round 3 finds the last basis. **Total miscode rate 0.54%** (per-line: 0, 0.80%, 1.61%, 0, 0, 0.80%). This is the paper's proof that the method survives contact with real, lossy market data.

### Runtime (Table IV)

Repeating each case 50Ã—, PCA runs in 0.002â€“0.08 s, and individual rounds up to ~1.22 s (the 200-bus Round-1 DPCP is the heaviest). **The full identification costs a few seconds or less in every case** â€” consistent with the $O(M^3)$ SVD being the practical bottleneck at these data sizes.

